<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dagobert I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 603–639</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dagobert I was a Merovingian king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, made king of Austrasia in 623 and ruler of all the Frankish kingdoms from 629 until his death in 639. With advisers such as Eligius he strengthened royal power, and he was a patron of the abbey of Saint-Denis, where he was the first king to be buried. After him real power passed increasingly to the mayors of the palace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dagobert-i.jpg" alt="Dagobert I" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tr%C3%A9missis_de_Dagobert_Ier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The church grew immensely rich through royal and aristocratic gifts, and monasteries multiplied, especially after the Irish monk Columbanus founded Luxeuil around 590; the abbey of Saint-Denis near Paris, where <b>Dagobert I</b> was buried in 639, became closely tied to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dagobert I</b>, king of Austrasia since 623, became ruler of Neustria and Burgundy as well, and so of all the Frankish kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The chronicle of Fredegar records that King Judicael met <b>Dagobert I</b> at Clichy in 636 and made peace with the Franks.</blockquote><a href="/state/domnonee">Domnonée</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 633 <b>King Dagobert I</b> appointed Radulf duke to defend the eastern frontier against the Slavs of Samo's Empire, then raiding Thuringia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-thuringia">Duchy of Thuringia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 631 a dispute over murdered Frankish merchants led <b>King Dagobert I</b> to invade; Samo defeated the Franks at Wogastisburg, and the Sorb prince Dervan then joined him.</blockquote><a href="/state/samos-empire">Samo's Empire</a></li></ul></section>
